---

<PageLayout
  title="About Dresden"
  lede="Baroque skyline, world-class culture and research, and a compact, green city on the Elbe."
  crumbs={[{ label: 'Home', href: '/' }, { label: 'Venue & Travel' }, { label: 'Local Information' }]}
>
  <Prose>
    <h2>Culture and history</h2>
    <p>
      Dresden enchants visitors with Baroque elegance and lively culture. With the Frauenkirche, the Zwinger
      and the Semperoper, it is one of Germany's most popular destinations, welcoming millions of guests
      every year, around 20 percent of them from abroad. More than 50 museums, including the Dresden State
      Art Collections (Staatliche Kunstsammlungen) with 14 museums, and more than 30 theatre stages shape the
      city's cultural life, alongside festivals such as the Dresden Music Festival, the Dixieland Festival
      and the Jazztage.
    </p>

    <h2>Science and innovation</h2>
    <p>
      Dresden stands for excellent research and innovation. TU Dresden has been one of Germany's
      Universities of Excellence since 2012, and the city is a leader in microelectronics, information and
      communication technology, nanotechnology, materials science and the life sciences, with research
      institutes and international companies working closely together.
    </p>

    <h2>Life in the city</h2>
    <p>
      Hardly a weekend passes without an event: open-air film nights on the banks of the Elbe, museum and
      theatre nights, and city festivals. The Äußere Neustadt is a lively district full of bars, cafés,
      restaurants, clubs and small theatres. Dresden is also one of Europe's greenest cities, with the Großer
      Garten, the Elbe meadows, the Dresdner Heide forest and vineyards on the slopes of the Elbe valley,
      even within the city limits.
    </p>

    <h2>Compact and well connected</h2>
    <p>
      Dresden is compact: famous sights are within walking distance of each other, and the airport is
      20 minutes away by public transport. The surroundings are close too: Meissen and its porcelain
      manufactory are 30 minutes away, and the sandstone landscapes of Saxon Switzerland less than
      40 minutes. Berlin and Prague are each about two hours away. Dresden, the capital of Saxony, has
      around 574,000 inhabitants.
    </p>

    <p class="text-sm text-[#66668F]">
      More visitor information:
      <a href="https://www.visit-dresden-elbland.de/" target="_blank" rel="noopener">visit-dresden-elbland.de</a>
      and <a href="https://www.dresden-convention.com/" target="_blank" rel="noopener">dresden-convention.com</a>.
      The exact conference venue will be announced soon.
    </p>
  </Prose>
